I'm trying to figure out if it's reliably addressed in .NET 6 or if it's just happening to typically not repro due to timing; I believe it's the latter, in which case there's probably still a fix to consider in .NET 6+.  I don't think this is related to channels at all, actually.  What's happening is the CTS's underlying timer fires and Cancel's the CTS, which synchronously completes the Task.Delay's task, which synchronously invokes the await continuation from that task, which then synchronously blocks in the Task.WaitAll.  The problem is there's another registration in the CTS waiting to be invoked, and it won't be invoked until after the first callback completes, and it won't complete until that Task.WaitAll completes, which won't complete until the other registration is invoked to cancel the task being waited on... deadlock.  You can see this with a simple repro:
```C#
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(2000);
cts.Token.Register(() => Console.WriteLine("B"));
try { await Task.Delay(-1, cts.Token); } catch { }
Console.WriteLine("A");
Console.ReadLine();
```
That will output "A" but then won't output "B" until you press enter.  The workaround is to break the synchronous continuation chain leading to blocking, and the easiest way to do that is to add an await that will be guaranteed to yield.  For example, in your repro, change the `catch (OperationCanceledException) { }` line to instead be `catch (OperationCanceledException) { await Task.Yield(); }` and you should see the issue go away.  Technically, then, things are behaving as designed and this is "user error", but it's also very subtle and we should consider avoiding the situation, likely via a change to Task.Delay that avoids running its continuations synchronously as part of completion due to cancellation.

